Let \( G \) be a group and let \( n = |G| \).

For each divisor \( d \) of \( n \) such that \( 1 < d \leq \sqrt{n} \), proceed as follows:

1. For every subset \( A \subseteq G \) of size \( d \) that contains the identity element \( 1 \), do the following:
2. For every subset \( B \subseteq G \) of size \( n/d \) such that \( 1 \in B \subseteq (G \setminus (A^{-1}A)) \cup \{1\} \):
3. Check whether \( G = AB \).

If such subsets \( A \) and \( B \) can always be found for all valid values of \( d \), then print:
" \( G \) is a CFS group."

Otherwise, print: " \( G \) is not a CFS group."
and also display the group \( G \), and the first subset \( A \) (along with its size \( d \)) for which the condition \( G = AB \) is not satisfied.

Here’s the code I’ve written for this:

```gap
IsCFSGroup := function(G)
  local n, divs, d, Asets, A, Bsize, Bsets, B, AinverseA, complement, product, success;
  n := Size(G);
  divs := Filtered(DivisorsInt(n), d -> d > 1 and d <= Sqrt(n));
  for d in divs do
    Asets := Filtered(Combinations(Elements(G), d), A -> Identity(G) in A);
    for A in Asets do
      AinverseA := Set(List(Cartesian(A, A), x -> x[1]^(-1)*x[2]));
      complement := Difference(Elements(G), AinverseA);
      AddSet(complement, Identity(G));  # Ensure 1 ∈ B
      Bsize := n / d;
      Bsets := Filtered(Combinations(complement, Bsize), B -> Identity(G) in B);

      success := false;
      for B in Bsets do
        product := Set(List(Cartesian(A, B), x -> x[1]*x[2]));
        if Set(product) = Set(Elements(G)) then
          success := true;
          break;
        fi;
      od;

      if not success then
        Print("G is not a CFS group.\n");
        Print("Violating subset A: ", A, "\n");
        Print("Size of A (d): ", d, "\n");
        return false;
      fi;
    od;
  od;
  Print("G is a CFS group.\n");
  return true;
end;
```

